WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.318
This is Make Your First Beats, by Monobox. Drum machines and rhythm, one step at a time.

00:00:07.118 --> 00:00:10.361
Today: swing and groove and accents and dynamics.

00:00:12.161 --> 00:00:15.482
Swing and groove. Why some beats make you move.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:30.527
A beat programmed exactly on the grid can sound stiff. Real drummers do not play like a clock: some notes land a little late, some a little early, and that is a large part of what people call groove. Drum machines imitate this with swing.

00:00:32.027 --> 00:00:33.104
What swing does.

00:00:33.904 --> 00:00:50.330
Swing delays every second step in each pair. Take the sixteenths of one beat, 1 e and a: with swing, the e and the a arrive a little later than the grid, while the 1 and the and stay where they are. The result is a long-short, long-short feel instead of an even one.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:01:07.458
Roger Linn, who designed the LM-1 Drum Computer and later the MPC, introduced swing and quantizing on the LM-1 in 1979. He has explained how his swing setting works: it delays the even-numbered sixteenths, and it is measured as a percentage.

00:01:08.258 --> 00:01:12.949
50% means no swing: both sixteenths in each pair get the same length.

00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:20.753
66% means perfect triplet swing: the first sixteenth gets two thirds of the time and the second gets one third.

00:01:21.553 --> 00:01:33.381
In between, and up to about 70%, there are many useful settings. Linn mentions that a light setting, around 54%, can loosen straight sixteenths without sounding obviously swung.

00:01:34.181 --> 00:01:44.915
Not every drum machine labels swing this way. Some use a 0 to 100 scale, or a knob with no numbers. The idea is the same: how late the off steps arrive.

00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:47.358
The arithmetic.

00:01:48.158 --> 00:02:08.545
At 120 BPM, a pair of sixteenths lasts 250 ms. With no swing, each gets 125 ms. At 66%, the first gets about 167 ms and the second about 83 ms. Small differences in time, but your body notices them.

00:02:10.045 --> 00:02:11.142
Where to use swing.

00:02:11.942 --> 00:02:15.712
Hip-hop and R&B often use clear swing on the hats and the kick.

00:02:16.512 --> 00:02:19.021
House often uses a light swing on the hats.

00:02:19.821 --> 00:02:23.662
Techno and trap are often straighter, but even a few percent can help.

00:02:24.462 --> 00:02:33.342
You can also swing some sounds and not others. A straight kick with swung hats is a very common combination, if your drum machine allows swing per track.

00:02:34.842 --> 00:02:36.139
Groove beyond swing.

00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:51.659
Swing is the most important timing tool, but not the only one. Moving a single snare a few milliseconds late can make a beat feel relaxed; moving it early makes it feel urgent. Some drum machines let you nudge individual steps off the grid for this.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:03:01.780
Tip: Judge swing with your body, not your eyes. Turn it up until it sounds too much, then back off until it feels natural. Nod your head to it.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.021
Practice.

00:03:04.821 --> 00:03:17.510
First: program the boom bap beat from the last chapter at 90 BPM. Play it with swing at 50%, then 54%, 58%, 62% and 66%.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:24.552
Second: make the hats play every step, and repeat. Swing is easier to hear on sixteenths.

00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:30.707
Third: if your machine has swing per track, swing only the hats and keep the kick straight.

00:03:32.507 --> 00:03:36.584
Accents and dynamics. Loud, soft and in between.

00:03:37.384 --> 00:03:48.778
Real drummers do not hit every note equally hard. Some hits are strong, some are barely there. Those differences, called dynamics, are what make a beat sound alive instead of mechanical.

00:03:50.278 --> 00:03:51.822
Velocity and accents.

00:03:52.622 --> 00:04:05.310
Most drum machines let you set how hard each step plays. It may be called velocity, level or accent. A lower value is softer, and on good drum machines often duller too, as on a real drum.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:12.944
The simplest use is on the hi-hats. Make the hats on the beats and the ands stronger, and the ones in between softer:

00:04:13.744 --> 00:04:15.798
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:16.598 --> 00:04:23.953
Here X means a loud hit and x a soft one. Even this small change makes sixteenth-note hats sound far less robotic.

00:04:25.453 --> 00:04:26.337
Ghost notes.

00:04:27.137 --> 00:04:39.268
Ghost notes are very soft snare hits between the main beats. You feel them more than you hear them. They fill the gaps and give the groove a rolling motion, and they are common in funk, hip-hop and drum and bass.

00:04:40.068 --> 00:04:42.122
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:42.922 --> 00:04:47.285
Here g is a ghost note: the same snare, at a much lower velocity.

00:04:48.785 --> 00:04:50.191
Variation over time.

00:04:50.991 --> 00:04:55.822
A pattern that repeats perfectly for minutes can get tiring. Small changes help:

00:04:56.622 --> 00:04:59.624
An extra kick or snare at the end of every fourth bar.

00:05:00.424 --> 00:05:03.888
An open hat instead of a closed one, once every two bars.

00:05:04.688 --> 00:05:12.154
Probability, on drum machines that have it: a step that plays only some of the time, for example on 50% of the repeats.

00:05:12.954 --> 00:05:20.124
Tip: If a beat feels flat, look at the hats first. Changing their dynamics is the fastest way to give a beat life.

00:05:21.624 --> 00:05:22.365
Practice.

00:05:23.165 --> 00:05:30.315
First: program sixteenth-note hats at a single velocity. Then make the offbeats softer, as in the first grid.

00:05:31.115 --> 00:05:37.066
Second: add the ghost-note snare pattern. Lower the ghost notes until you feel them more than hear them.

00:05:37.866 --> 00:05:44.654
Third: if your machine has probability, give a few hat or kick steps 50% and listen to the variations.

00:05:46.454 --> 00:05:47.543
That's all for today.

00:05:48.343 --> 00:05:49.568
If you want to keep going:

00:05:50.368 --> 00:05:59.307
Monobox is the app behind the ebook. It is a drum machine and groovebox: a place to make beats, step by step, and turn them into grooves.

00:06:00.107 --> 00:06:02.949
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be tried with it:

00:06:03.749 --> 00:06:05.731
programming patterns on a step grid.

00:06:06.531 --> 00:06:08.663
knowing what each drum sound does.

00:06:09.463 --> 00:06:11.773
building the classic rhythms of each genre.

00:06:12.573 --> 00:06:15.463
adding swing, dynamics and sound design.

00:06:16.263 --> 00:06:18.196
turning a pattern into a whole track.

00:06:18.996 --> 00:06:23.527
